Investigate a lesson through contrasting responses and independent transfer (@inquiry-learning-study: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Hand-over-a-practical-method-and-make-its-limits-observable.lingua#r_003F2F980FE4FCF88BC8DFD75D") { r_0027293C2A3873361E52F78551
Assemble a teaching and assessment dossier for the capacity-versus-feasibility idea. Its purpose is to make the explanation, learner demands and inference inspectable. It contains original task materials and hypothetical response analyses, not a performed educational experiment. Use the existing engineering model and its independent scheduling checks where useful; do not rewrite the production solver as a learning model.

Start with the four prerequisite demonstrations in [Diagnose preparation and explain a dependency before naming a rule](Diagnose-preparation-and-explain-a-dependency-before-naming-a-rule.lingua#r_00A948449B90BC0C5A2F092202). Record actual attempts if a learner participates; otherwise label the entries as predicted responses. Present case F as a worked example, ask for a reconstruction with the example hidden, then use case O as a prediction and counterexample. Provide targeted feedback and preserve the first attempt. Use D and U for independent practice with declared help. The solutions and rubric are available for self-study, so a later claim of unaided performance must record whether they were consulted.

The two different ways of solving the original task are a construction and a bound. A construction lists station-slot assignments and verifies all dependencies and conflicts. A bound identifies a necessary condition on every possible schedule. Combine them to establish an optimum. For the four cases the attainable values and bounds coincide. Enumerating a finite model checks the authored key; it does not establish that the explanation teaches the method.

Use this changed transfer task after a declared delay, keeping its solution covered until the attempt is recorded. A single paper exhibit requires a drawing D and a caption C, which may be prepared independently, followed by assembly S. Each task occupies one whole slot and completed inputs become available at the next boundary. D and C can each be done in slot 0 on separate stations. Assembly is initially available only in slot 0. Is completion by the end of slot 1 possible? No: S at 0 precedes completion of both prerequisites. If S is also available in slot 1, choose D and C at 0 and S at 1; completion at time 2 is feasible. This is a fork-and-join dependency rather than two copies of a serial job.

Ask the learner to identify what transfers: counts still provide necessary resource bounds, but precedence must be respected and a witness establishes feasibility. Ask what changed: assembly requires two different completed inputs and the drawing/caption tasks can run concurrently. Renaming A and B would have been a weaker change. Even success here supports transfer only within this bounded family of discrete dependency problems; it does not establish transfer to every engineering decision.

Next change the transfer rule: assembly may start only one full slot after both inputs finish. With D and C in slot 0, S in slot 1 is now too early, and S in slot 2 would be the earliest possible. Have the learner revise the prior conclusion rather than defend it unchanged. An adequate explanation identifies the changed premise, the invalidated schedule and a revised feasible opportunity. This checks sensitivity to assumptions as well as remembered output.

Keep a dossier with the capability claim, prerequisite responses, first prediction, assisted reconstruction, independent attempts, delays/intervening work, criterion vectors, feedback, transfer reasoning and revision. Compare your interpretation with a critical reader's alternative. If there is no other participant, complete the source analysis and self-study dossier but leave interpersonal teaching and independent learner observations unperformed. Do not invent them to fill a table.

**Independent extension:** choose a different shared core idea, such as [Design comparisons that can distinguish competing explanations](Design-comparisons-that-can-distinguish-competing-explanations.lingua#r_0A7E63167B1C0C05400C73A84E) and its length/time confounding example. Create an explanation, two contrasting predictions, an assessment that distinguishes plausible rival reasoning, a feedback response and a changed transfer task. Defend the inference from evidence to capability. Have another reader challenge the task and revise it; record whether that review actually occurred. This demands subject understanding and instructional judgment beyond reproducing our four schedules.

**Author verification:** all 64 three-slot availability calendars were checked by independent operation-pair enumeration against the unchanged shared production solver. The four keys, schedule witnesses, distinguishing task subsets, transfer timing, correlation counterexample, missing-score bounds and published-mean subtractions were also checked. These checks establish the stated finite accounts, not psychological validity or completed teaching. No new learning-effect simulator is supplied.

**Continue:** [Hand over a practical method and make its limits observable](Hand-over-a-practical-method-and-make-its-limits-observable.lingua#r_003F2F980FE4FCF88BC8DFD75D).

